            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance rover news is that NASA/JPL reported on Aug. 5, 2026, that Perseverance captured a first-of-its-kind observation from Mars: Earth disappearing behind the Martian moon Phobos. The rover’s Mastcam-Z camera took the image sequence on July 2, 2026, during sol 1,907 of the mission, from the rim of Jezero Crater. NASA describes it as the first time Earth has been observed “vanishing” behind another object from the surface of another planet 【5:0†source】.\n\nA few other recent Perseverance updates:\n\n- NASA also posted the composite image as a featured Perseverance item on Aug. 5, showing Earth as a tiny bright dot passing behind Phobos 【5:3†source】.\n- In mid-July, NASA reported that Perseverance found evidence that a 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of ancient rocks at Jezero’s rim was formed by repeated asteroid impacts, in an area called the “Broom Point member” 【5:2†source】.\n- Perseverance recently passed a driving milestone: it completed the equivalent of a marathon on Mars, reaching 26.2 miles / 42.195 km of total driving on sol 1,890, much faster than Opportunity reached the same distance 【5:4†source】.",
                "annotations": [
